[Music]  how would you go about fixing this  wobbly bridge  [Music]  how about changing this grid pattern to  make it symmetrical  when people solve problems typically  they  think well what can i add that will make  it better wait also left to right  i see there's a couple of green boxes i  need to change i could have got this is  not i should have taken  we tend not to ask ourselves what can we  take away from  it what can we subtract what can we  remove that might still make something  better  lydia klotz is an engineer who's  fascinated by the psychology  of design he started to notice  that people's first instinct is often to  add  one real epiphany was playing legos  actually with my son ezra who's about  two and a half at the time and we were  building a lego  bridge basically and so the two columns  on the bridge one was longer than the  other one  and so to make the bridge level i turned  around to  grab a block to add to the shorter  column  and by the time i had turned around ezra  had just removed a block from the longer  column  lydie realized that in many cases  removing an element  actually leads to better design one  example that we love  is the balance bikes the pedal-less  miniature bikes that allow two-year-olds  to ride  if you've seen a little two-year-old  going on these things i mean it just  makes you disappointed that you didn't  have it  when you were a kid but the breakthrough  was just taking away the pedals  it took decades for someone to think of  removing the pedals  rather than adding stabilizers  and yet through history people have  championed different kinds of minimalism  people are familiar with marie kondo  right tidying your home but  you know going back you think uh  leonardo da vinci his famous  definition of perfection is um when  there's nothing left to take away and  my personal favorite is wow sue which is  i'm paraphrasing here but to gain  knowledge add things every day to gain  wisdom  subtract things every day and what's  interesting about this is  all throughout history there have been  these reminders but the fact that we  need a reminder is  some of the best evidence that our  brains don't think of it  on their own lydia's been working with a  team of behavioral scientists  to try and figure out why our brains  default to adding  and what if anything can be done to  change this  so in our first study we showed people a  lego structure that looked like this  and the instructions were to improve the  structure so that it could hold a brick  above a small figurine most of the  participants  added bricks for support that is unless  the experimenters reminded people that  they could add  or remove bricks half of participants  were told that each piece that you add  costs 10 cents and the other half of  participants  were told that each piece that you add  costs 10 cents but removing  pieces is free so when we reminded  participants that removing pieces is  free  participants were much more likely to  remove pieces from the structure  than when we didn't remind them without  reminders  only 41 of people took away the extra  brick  but when queued 61 went with the cheaper  solution  could it be that taking things away just  doesn't occur to people  gabrielle wanted to know what other  factors affect this behavior  for example what if people tried the  task while distracted by something else  we gave them a 10 by 10 grid during the  task you'll be given a set of patterns  change each pattern so it's perfectly  symmetrical from left to right  and from top to bottom subtracting would  require fewer clicks  than adding and the instructions were to  use as few clicks as possible but there  was another task that participants were  asked to do  at the same time right press  f every time you see a five  [Laughter]  there's another five okay if there's  another five i've not even got a time to  start the task two  fives oh lots of fives um  finally i think i've got it okay done  what we find  is that when they are under what we call  cognitive load  so when they are distracted when their  attention is taken up by another task  that's when it tends not to occur to  people  that they could solve the task by  removing those extra squares  i could have got this is not i should  have taken  idea let me retreat  when people have more time or attention  or cognitive resources  they're more likely to find the more  efficient solution  but adding things was still the default  response across a range of examples and  conditions studied  so this is a novel heuristic heuristics  are  basically mental shortcuts that people  take when they process  any kind of information and so by  identifying  one way in which that information  processing  is biased that it's biased toward  additive  solutions and not thinking of  subtractive ones  i really think that we're we're on to  something kind of new here  gabrielle and the team think that this  previously unrecognized  bias in thinking could have big  implications when we overlook  subtraction  that's when we get things like  overburdened schedules  busy people increasing red tape and  institutions  and we're missing out on opportunities  right opportunities to make our lives  more fulfilling institutions more  effective  institutions more efficient and even to  make the planet better  more livable more green more sustainable

